    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline, lede and decision block say what to do: ship none yet. They say why: the only two configurations that hold 97% in every task, Brisk-S high with a retry and Atlas-M high with a retry, read overall p95s of 870 ms and 2551 ms against a 600 ms budget. They also name the main risks: per-task latency was never read, the hold-side configuration Brisk-S low with a retry could flip on a rerun, and Brisk-S high with a retry clears multi-hop by only 0.3 points. c1 then shows the per-task pass-rate picture."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 2, "reason": "c1 is the pass-rate filter, c2 the latency filter and c3 shows that nothing under the budget clears every task. c4 compares the two survivors. Each sentence matches its table. c1's multi-hop rows and c3's ten under-budget configurations all sit below 97% on multi-hop. c3's 95.6%, 96.4% and 97.0% match the gate figures. c2 rests on the fact that a configuration's slowest task is at least as slow as its overall p95, which holds if the tasks split the cases. The code also reads the two survivors as undecided only on latency."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 2, "reason": "Watch gives Brisk-S high with a retry's thin margins (0.3, 0.6 and 0.9 points; 37.4%, 29.9% and 16.5% wrong-side odds on assumed counts). It also gives the hold-side margins of Brisk-S low with a retry (11.9%, 33.3% and 50%, which I confirmed). c4 says the two survivors' pass rates are not told apart (-0.7, interval -1.7 to +0.4). The cost ratio is limited to this run's unnamed routing."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "None of the omitted items would move the hold. The Nano-XS configurations miss every task. The overall pass rate and the grid effects do not decide the gates. p50 and calls per task are not gated. The gate sweep from 94% to 98% never changes the call, because latency is unread for every arm."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "The page carries the unread per-task latency, the unstated per-task counts (with the assumption stated each time), best-of-18, the missing control and the unnamed provider routing. Each appears in the story, the confidence clauses and the next steps. The confidence clauses say what would make each caveat matter and why the hold stands until then."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names a read, a declaration or a rerun, with case counts I confirmed (1,685 and 7,567 for Brisk-S low with a retry), and says what it would settle. One overreach is noted below."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 2, "reason": "c1 and c3 draw their per-task rates against the 97% line. c2 draws the overall p50-to-p95 ranges against the budget, and its caption says the gate is per task. c4 draws both the cost bars and the interval on the pass-rate gap, with the rounding note. c3's example caption now matches its order and says the examples show kinds of failure, not shares."}
